MITRE ATT&CK – TA0042: Resource Development

Golden Rule

I'm going to give you one sentence that I want you to remember forever:

Reconnaissance = Learn about the victim. 🕵️

Resource Development = Prepare your own resources. 🧰

That's it.

TA0042: Resource Development is one of the 14 tactics in the MITRE ATT&CK® framework. 
It sits at the very beginning of the adversary lifecycle—before any actual targeting or intrusion occurs.

What It Means
Resource Development covers the activities an adversary undertakes to create, purchase, or compromise the infrastructure, 
accounts, and capabilities they'll need for later operations. Think of it as the "pre-production" phase of an attack campaign.

If an attacker wants to phish a target, they first need:
A domain to host the fake login page
An email account to send from
Possibly malware that evades known signatures

All of that preparation falls under Resource Development.


Why It Matters for Defenders

Resource Development is often the only phase where the adversary is visible before the attack. Indicators include:

Domain registrations with names similar to your brand (typosquats)
Certificate transparency logs showing new TLS certs for lookalike domains
Social media profiles impersonating your executives
Dark web marketplace activity selling or buying access to your infrastructure


Defensive Actions

Threat Intelligence: Monitor for domains, certs, and accounts impersonating your organization
Brand Protection: Use services that detect typosquats and fake social profiles
Supply Chain Monitoring: Watch for compromised infrastructure in your vendor ecosystem
Honeypots & Deception: Deploy fake credentials or infrastructure to detect when adversaries "test" their tools


Real-World Context

Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) and ransomware groups spend weeks or months in Resource Development. For example:

